last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.69\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.34 to +1.05) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.62\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.20 to +1.04). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in none. Highs rose in none; 4 months fell.","t_rain":"Dayton has gotten wetter","p_rain":"Dayton's yearly precipitation has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.38 inches per decade</strong> (95% range +0.06 to +0.71), about +4.9 inches since 1896. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 41.3 inches a year. The wettest year was 1990 (59.75 in); the driest, 1934 (23.74 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 8 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}